    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe placement is right: `generateCustomerCode` stays in the service because it needs the repository to count, while the pure derivations stay as static helpers on `Owner`. The per-city count correctly reuses the established `findAll().stream().filter(existing -> !Objects.equals(existing.getId(), owner.getId()))...` idiom used by `countNamesakes`/`assertTelephoneIsUnique`/`assertHouseholdIsUnique`, and letting `Owner.membershipNumber` compose on top of the raw `customerCode` (only its javadoc example updated) is the natural, minimal way to rebuild derived values. One structural nit:\n\n- **Duplicated 3-char-prefix transform in `generateCustomerCode` (ClinicServiceImpl.java:288\u2013289).** The expression `X.substring(0, Math.min(3, X.length())).toUpperCase()` is now written twice verbatim (for `city3` and `last3`). The non-trivial truncation guard is exactly the kind of logic you don't want duplicated and independently maintained. This codebase already has a clear habit of factoring such string transforms into named helpers (`Owner.normalizeEmail`, `normalizeTelephone`, `normalizeAddress`, `normalizeHousehold`). Extract a small private helper, e.g. `String codePrefix(String value)` returning the upper-cased first-three, and call it for both `city3` and `last3`. That removes the duplication and matches the surrounding convention.",
